WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.689
Here are two glasses.

00:00:01.689 --> 00:00:04.202
One holds juice, the other holds milk.

00:00:04.202 --> 00:00:10.420
Your job is to swap them, so the juice ends up in the second glass, and the milk in the first.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.184
Pour the milk into the first glass?

00:00:13.184 --> 00:00:14.770
Then the juice is gone.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.567
You need a third, empty glass.

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:19.515
Keep that picture in mind.

00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:23.560
Python has the same problem, and two ways to solve it.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:28.973
This is part 3 of Lecture 2: assignment and arithmetic.

00:00:29.323 --> 00:00:37.043
We'll look at assignment more closely, swap two values, and then learn the rules Python uses for arithmetic.

00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:40.792
Remember the rule for an assignment.

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:45.393
Evaluate the right side, then bind the name on the left.

00:00:45.993 --> 00:00:51.593
The right side can be any expression, and it can use the very name being assigned.

00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:53.509
x is bound to 6.

00:00:53.509 --> 00:01:00.063
Then x becomes 100 minus 10, plus x times 3, minus x divided by 10.

00:01:00.413 --> 00:01:08.981
The whole right side is evaluated first, using the old x, which is 6.

00:01:08.981 --> 00:01:13.948
That's 100 minus 10, plus 18, minus 0.6.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:14.693
107.4.

00:01:15.293 --> 00:01:19.853
Only then is x rebound, to 107.4.

00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:20.990
One warning.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:26.629
The slides write this example with a long dash, the way PowerPoint does automatically.

00:01:26.629 --> 00:01:29.317
Python only accepts the plain minus sign.

00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:32.923
Copy the slide into Python, and you get a syntax error.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.615
Here's an example from the slides, drawn as an environment diagram.

00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:38.900
Let's step through it.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.690
x is bound to 1.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.130
y is bound to 2.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.656
Then x becomes y times 2.

00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:49.661
Python follows the arrow of y, finds 2, and computes 4.

00:01:49.661 --> 00:01:52.300
Then it moves the arrow of x.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.673
Now the object 1 has no name left.

00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:58.250
The program prints 4, then 2.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.430
One statement can also bind several names at once.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.574
This is cascaded assignment.

00:02:04.574 --> 00:02:07.944
The expression is evaluated once, giving 11,

00:02:07.944 --> 00:02:12.080
and then all three names are bound to that one object.

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:14.129
Now, the swap.

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:16.654
a is bound to 1, and b to 2.

00:02:16.654 --> 00:02:18.907
We want to exchange them.

00:02:19.257 --> 00:02:21.460
Here's the obvious attempt.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.765
Pause the video.

00:02:22.765 --> 00:02:25.457
Draw the diagram after each line.

00:02:25.457 --> 00:02:29.617
What does it print, and where does the object 1 go?

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.010
Let's see.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.021
a becomes b.

00:02:32.021 --> 00:02:37.076
Now both names point at 2, and nothing points at 1 any more.

00:02:37.076 --> 00:02:39.687
It's lost, just like the juice.

00:02:40.037 --> 00:02:41.707
Then b becomes a.

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:45.637
That binds b to 2, which it already was.

00:02:45.987 --> 00:02:48.976
So the program prints 2, 2.

00:02:48.976 --> 00:02:50.747
The swap failed.

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:54.070
The fix is the empty third glass.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:58.374
Before you change a, keep a third name on its old value.

00:02:58.724 --> 00:03:00.107
temp is bound to a.

00:03:00.107 --> 00:03:02.364
Now the object 1 has two names.

00:03:02.714 --> 00:03:03.956
a becomes b.

00:03:03.956 --> 00:03:07.474
The 1 is still safe, held by temp.

00:03:07.824 --> 00:03:09.413
b becomes temp.

00:03:09.413 --> 00:03:12.377
And the program prints 2, 1.

00:03:12.377 --> 00:03:13.224
Swapped.

00:03:14.001 --> 00:03:17.685
Python has a shorter way, called simultaneous assignment.

00:03:17.685 --> 00:03:21.563
The right side is a list of expressions separated by commas,

00:03:21.563 --> 00:03:24.601
and the left side has the same number of names.

00:03:24.951 --> 00:03:29.178
Here are two names bound to two strings.

00:03:29.178 --> 00:03:31.503
Now look at this line.

00:03:31.503 --> 00:03:33.511
c, o, becomes o, c.

00:03:33.861 --> 00:03:36.789
It happens in two complete phases.

00:03:36.789 --> 00:03:41.094
First, every expression on the right is evaluated.

00:03:41.094 --> 00:03:45.141
Then the names on the left are bound, in order.

00:03:45.741 --> 00:03:50.577
Both old objects are fetched before either name moves, so nothing is lost.

00:03:50.577 --> 00:03:52.341
The two names have swapped.

00:03:52.691 --> 00:03:56.132
That's a pattern worth remembering.

00:03:56.132 --> 00:04:00.851
To swap two variables, write a, b, becomes b, a.

00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:03.962
But don't use it just to save lines.

00:04:03.962 --> 00:04:08.636
Two unrelated calculations on one line are legal, but hard to read.

00:04:08.636 --> 00:04:12.611
Two separate assignments say the same thing more clearly.

00:04:13.401 --> 00:04:14.804
Now, arithmetic.

00:04:14.804 --> 00:04:17.783
Python has 7 arithmetic operators.

00:04:17.783 --> 00:04:21.201
Here they are, each applied to 7 and 2.

00:04:21.551 --> 00:04:25.759
Plus, minus and times work as you'd expect.

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:27.031
9, 5, and 14.

00:04:27.381 --> 00:04:31.301
A single slash is division, and gives 3.5.

00:04:31.651 --> 00:04:35.584
A double slash is floor division, and gives 3.

00:04:35.584 --> 00:04:38.746
Percent is the remainder, which is 1.

00:04:38.746 --> 00:04:40.884
And double star is power.

00:04:40.884 --> 00:04:42.251
7 squared is 49.

00:04:43.035 --> 00:04:48.431
When an expression has several operators, Python needs to know which one to do first.

00:04:48.431 --> 00:04:51.035
The rules are called operator precedence.

00:04:51.385 --> 00:04:54.502
Parentheses always come first.

00:04:54.502 --> 00:04:55.646
Then power.

00:04:55.646 --> 00:05:00.946
Then times, division, floor division and remainder.

00:05:00.946 --> 00:05:03.025
Then plus and minus.

00:05:03.625 --> 00:05:06.649
Operators on the same level are done left to right.

00:05:06.649 --> 00:05:08.785
It's the order you know from school.

00:05:09.135 --> 00:05:11.169
Let's evaluate this one.

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:14.135
Power first: 2 to the power 3 is 8.

00:05:14.485 --> 00:05:18.722
Then division and times, left to right.

00:05:18.722 --> 00:05:21.112
8 divided by 4 is 2.0.

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:23.285
2.0 times 5 is 10.0.

00:05:23.635 --> 00:05:26.264
Last, 1 plus 10.0 is 11.0.

00:05:26.264 --> 00:05:30.715
It's a float, because the division made one.

00:05:31.065 --> 00:05:33.238
Two surprises with power.

00:05:33.238 --> 00:05:39.149
It groups from the right, so 2 to the 3 to the 2 is 2 to the 9, 512.

00:05:39.149 --> 00:05:43.582
And power binds tighter than a minus sign in front.

00:05:43.582 --> 00:05:46.625
Minus 2, double star 2, is minus 4.

00:05:46.975 --> 00:05:48.805
When in doubt, add parentheses.

00:05:48.805 --> 00:05:53.055
They cost nothing, and they save your reader from remembering the table.

00:05:53.835 --> 00:05:57.299
Floor division divides, then rounds down to a whole number.

00:05:57.299 --> 00:06:00.235
And the remainder operator gives what's left over.

00:06:00.585 --> 00:06:06.833
257 seconds, floor divided by 60, is 4 complete minutes.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:11.185
And 257 mod 60 is 17 seconds left over.

00:06:11.535 --> 00:06:14.534
With two ints, floor division gives an int.

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:19.975
If either one is a float, the result is a float, with nothing after the point.

00:06:20.325 --> 00:06:21.599
Now be careful.

00:06:21.599 --> 00:06:26.525
Round down means towards minus infinity, not towards zero.

00:06:27.125 --> 00:06:31.063
Minus 2 divided by 3 is about minus 0.67.

00:06:31.063 --> 00:06:34.714
The next whole number down is minus 1.

00:06:34.714 --> 00:06:38.845
So minus 2, floor divided by 3, is minus 1.

00:06:39.195 --> 00:06:44.202
But int of minus 2 over 3 is 0, because int cuts towards zero.

00:06:44.202 --> 00:06:47.675
For negative numbers, they're not the same.

00:06:48.025 --> 00:06:51.044
Python keeps the two operators consistent.

00:06:51.044 --> 00:06:55.502
The quotient times b, plus the remainder, always gives back a.

00:06:55.502 --> 00:06:59.025
With b positive, the remainder is never negative.

00:06:59.802 --> 00:07:03.194
You often need both the quotient and the remainder.

00:07:03.194 --> 00:07:05.922
Divmod gives you both at once, as a pair.

00:07:06.272 --> 00:07:09.039
Divmod of 257 and 60 is 4 and 17.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:16.672
Unpack the pair with simultaneous assignment, and you have minutes and seconds in one line.

00:07:17.022 --> 00:07:19.815
Changing a variable by some amount is so common

00:07:19.815 --> 00:07:23.262
that Python has a short form, called augmented assignment.

00:07:23.612 --> 00:07:25.810
x is bound to 22.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:30.984
Plus equals 7 means, x becomes x plus 7.

00:07:30.984 --> 00:07:31.372
29.

00:07:31.722 --> 00:07:36.732
Minus equals 2 times 7 means x becomes x minus 14.

00:07:36.732 --> 00:07:40.741
The whole right side is evaluated first.

00:07:40.741 --> 00:07:41.042
15.

00:07:41.392 --> 00:07:46.276
This gives us a pattern you'll use in every loop from Lecture 3 on.

00:07:46.276 --> 00:07:49.630
Start a total at 0, then add each price to it.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:51.672
It's called the accumulator.

00:07:52.469 --> 00:07:53.829
Let's sum up.

00:07:54.179 --> 00:07:58.605
An assignment evaluates the whole right side first.

00:07:58.605 --> 00:08:04.941
Simultaneous assignment evaluates everything on the right before binding,

00:08:04.941 --> 00:08:07.459
so a, b, becomes b, a, swaps.

00:08:07.809 --> 00:08:10.570
Precedence: parentheses, then power,

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:15.249
then times and divisions, then plus and minus, left to right.

00:08:15.599 --> 00:08:19.637
Floor division rounds down, towards minus infinity.

00:08:19.637 --> 00:08:23.439
Percent is the remainder, and divmod gives both.

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:25.622
Here's one to try.

00:08:25.622 --> 00:08:32.652
Read a number of seconds, and print it as hours, minutes and seconds.

00:08:32.652 --> 00:08:35.097
3725 seconds is 1, 2, 5.

00:08:35.097 --> 00:08:36.829
Use divmod twice.

00:08:37.379 --> 00:08:43.059
In part four, our programs will start talking to the user, with input and print.

